"\n\n\n\n\n## Ordered\n\n\n## Review\nAfter the tour ended, I picked the most appealing spot from the guide's restaurant recommendations.\n\nIt was cold and I was craving something exotic with a warm broth.  \nSong Fa Bak Kut Teh from Singapore came to mind, and I figured a Thai restaurant would have something in a similar vein.\n\nIt's located in a basement, but once inside it was surprisingly spacious.  \nThe robot serving system was a cool modern touch.\n\nOrders are placed via QR code at the table and payment is handled instantly — no language barrier at all.  \nThe only slightly annoying part was having to register a card upfront before paying.  \nTravel Wallet cards are supported, and if registering is too much hassle, you can request a separate payment at the counter.\n\nThe Pad Thai was the richest and most flavorful I've ever had.  \nThe local prawns were big and plump — probably why.\n\nThe Spicy Beef Tendon Soup tasted familiar, like Korean galbitang, but with a slightly sour edge to the broth.  \nIt didn't bother me at all — I actually liked it.  \nStill not sure why it's called .\n\nOverall a satisfying meal.  \nIt's a large 150-seat restaurant that's popular with Thai locals too.  \n## Pictures\n\n\n\n\n"